{
  "image_name": "e75723e4aadd1f70ad3707c0d777b2bc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes strategies for making high-resolution, full-colour perovskite display pixels and integrating them with driving circuits. Overall, it shows how red, green, and blue emissive units can be patterned by inkjet printing while improving charge injection and shrinking feature size from microscale to quantum-scale dimensions.\n\nOn the left, a display stack is illustrated with transparent and injection layers surrounding patterned RGB pixels. A key point is that simply combining different colours can create mismatched energy alignment, so an added modifying layer appears to help tune the interface between the emissive material and charge-transport layers. Multi-nozzle inkjet printing is shown depositing separate RGB subpixels onto a substrate, suggesting scalable fabrication of colour arrays.\n\nThe right side emphasizes circuit integration. Perovskite material is combined with a self-assembled monolayer for electrode modification, then printed into confined structures on an IC backplane. This implies precise placement of emissive material directly onto electronic addressing units, which is important for active-matrix displays.\n\nThe bottom panel highlights miniaturization: patterned structures decrease from micrometre-sized blocks to nanoscale features and finally to sub-10 nm quantum-scale elements using templates, underscoring the push toward ever smaller, denser display pixels.",
